UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter24-peasant-and-tribal-movements-in-telangana-p1", "partName": "Peasant Rebellions in Telangana", "bodyMd": "Peasant unrest across Telangana in the pre-Independence decades grew out of a single underlying condition: landlords (Jagirdars, Deshmukhs, Zamindars, and Maktadars) extracted illegal taxes, forced labour (Vetti), and land from the peasantry, and local agitations broke out wherever this exploitation became unbearable.\n\n### Kolanpaka Peasants Agitation\n\n*   Kolanpaka village lies near Alair in Bhuvanagiri Taluq.\n*   **Andhra Maha Sabha leaders from this village:** Arutla Lakshmi Narsimha Reddy and Arutla Ram Chandra Reddy.\n*   The Kolanpaka Jagirdar, Nawab Turaj Yar Jung, collected illegal taxes from farmers and forced them into Vetti (unpaid labour).\n*   The Jagirdar also ran a programme of forcibly converting Dalits in the village to Islam. The Arutla brothers opposed this, brought in Arya Samaj members, and re-converted the affected Dalits to Hinduism through the Shuddhi programme.\n*   The forced religious conversions carried out by the Jagirdar were the main reason for the agitation of the Kolanpaka peasants.\n\n### Bethavolu Peasants Agitation\n\n*   Bethavolu was a Zamindari region, a cluster of Makta villages, then in Huzur Nagar Taluq of Nalgonda district (now in Suryapet district).\n*   **Bethavolu Maktadar:** Tadakamalla Sitha Ramchandra Rao.\n*   The collection of illegal and exorbitant taxes was the important reason behind the revolt of farmers and villagers.\n\n**Taxes imposed by the Bethavolu Maktadar:**\n\n| No. | Tax |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Cents Tax |\n| 2 | Pullari Tax |\n| 3 | God Tax |\n| 4 | Marriage Tax |\n| 5 | Relatives Tax |\n| 6 | Adoption Tax |\n| 7 | Illarikam Tax |\n| 8 | Adabidda Gampalu (Baskets) |\n| 9 | Koulu Pannu (Tenant Tax) |\n| 10 | Maggam Pannu (Weavers Tax) |\n| 11 | Manthrasani Pannu (Midwife Tax) |\n| 12 | Santhana Pannu |\n| 13 | Prostitute Tax |\n\n*   The agitating peasants'' demands were abolition of these illegal taxes and rights over the land.\n\n**Key roles:**  \n\n*   Suryapet Pleader Amrutha Reddy fought the case in favour of the peasants.\n*   Vemulapalli Venkata Krishna fought on behalf of the Zamindar.\n*   Bethavolu Patwari Pragada Raj Gopala Rao sided with the peasants.\n\n### Shaik Bandagi''s Land Agitation against the Visnoor Deshmukh\n\n*   The Visnoor Deshmukh of Janagaon Taluq, Rapaka Ramchandra Reddy, was known for cruelty and committed many atrocities on the people.\n*   Shaik Bandagi, a poor Muslim peasant of Kamareddy Gudem village, had four brothers (five including himself); the eldest, Abbas Ali, worked as a clerk under Ramachandra Reddy while the rest cultivated land.\n\n**Sequence of events:**  \n\n1.  The five brothers divided their land; Abbas Ali, being the eldest, received 8 acres more.\n2.  Abbas Ali later sold off his own share and tried to annex his brothers'' land.\n3.  Shaik Bandagi protested on his brothers'' behalf; a panchayat was held before the Deshmukh, who ruled in favour of Abbas Ali.\n4.  Bandagi then approached the court. The Deshmukh, angered, humiliated and threatened him.\n5.  Bandagi persisted and won the court judgment in his favour.\n6.  The humiliated Deshmukh conspired with Abbas Ali to kill Bandagi.\n7.  On 26 July 1940, while Bandagi was travelling to Hyderabad to collect the judgment copy, the Deshmukh''s goons murdered him near a bus stop.\n\n*   Bandagi''s brothers and friends buried him at the site, where a tomb was built; his death anniversary (Urus/Ursu) is observed there every year.\n*   This incident became a major inspiration for the Telangana Armed Struggle, and the play _Maa Bhoomi_ opens with a tribute to Shaik Bandagi.\n\n### Munugodu Peasants Revolt\n\n*   Munugodu village was in Nalgonda Taluq; its Deshmukh was Kancharla Rami Reddy, who harassed the general public.\n*   While travelling to Nalgonda in a bullock cart, the Deshmukh was stopped, killed, and his cart set ablaze by the people.\n\n### Paritala Peasants Struggle\n\n*   The Paritala Jagirdar was Nawab Kamal Yar Jung, who harassed people in his Jagir and extracted money through illegal taxes.\n*   The Paritala Jagir villages were not administratively connected to either the Nizam government or British-ruled Andhra.\n*   The Jagirdar''s agent, Lanka Subramaniya Shastri, tortured the people in various ways; the people revolted and killed him.\n\n### Other Landlord Agitations\n\nSimilar agitations against landlords also took place in the following villages:\n\n| Village | Landlord/Zamindar |\n|---|---|\n| Ammula Palem | Kalluri Rajeshwar Rao |\n| Mella Cheruvu | Chennuru Veera Bhadra Rao |\n| Bakkamanthula Gudem | Boghala Veera Reddy |\n| Errabadu | Jinna Reddy Pratap Reddy |\n| Dharmapuram (Zamindari) | Vekuri Raghav Rao |\n| Mundrai | Kadari Narsimha Rao |\n| Mulkala Gudem | Pingali Ranga Reddy |\n\n*   In Dharmapuram and Mundrai villages of Jangoan Taluq, Lambada tribal people held 200 acres of land that was taken over by landlords. With the help of the Andhra Mahasabha and the Communists, the Lambadas protested and reclaimed their land.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter24-peasant-and-tribal-movements-in-telangana-p2", "partName": "Tribal Rebellions in Telangana", "bodyMd": "Tribals traditionally had an inseparable relationship with the forests they lived in. With colonial and Nizam-era forest administration, the landlords and merchants of the plains began exploiting tribal communities in forest regions, and the state progressively curtailed tribal rights over forest land.\n\n### Forest Policy and Its Impact on Tribals\n\n*   Salar Jung established the Forest Department in Hyderabad State for the first time in 1867; forest policies followed in 1890 and 1900, expanding government rights over forests.\n*   With this, the traditional rights of tribals over the forests were abolished — Podu (shifting) cultivation in reserved forests and the collection of forest produce were both prohibited.\n*   **Effect:** major changes in the social and economic condition of tribal communities.\n*   Under the 1917 Act brought by the Nizam government:\n*   Tribals had to pay \"Bancherai Pannu\" and \"Pay Dumpa Patti\" for grazing cattle in the forest and for collecting firewood respectively.\n*   Ghar Patti, Nagar Patti, Fasal Patti, and Choubina were further taxes levied on tribal people.\n*   Tribals were additionally forced to pay various illegal taxes and fines to forest officials, failing which false cases were filed against them.\n*   Frustrated by these Nizam-era laws, the Gond community of Adilabad revolted under Ramji Gond and later under Komaram Bheem, against the interference of officials and non-tribals, demanding tribal control over forests.\n\n### Ramji Gond Rebellion (1857–60)\n\n*   Ramji Gond, active from around 1851–60, was the first tribal freedom fighter to resist British rule, making Jangaon (Asifabad) in Adilabad district his centre.\n*   During the 1857 Sepoy Mutiny, a large body of Rohilla soldiers under Tantia Tope entered Adilabad and made Nirmal their centre to fight the British.\n*   From 1857–60, Gond tribals and Rohilla Muslims of Adilabad rebelled together under Ramji Gond and Haji Rohilla.\n*   Ramji Gond raised and trained an army of Marathas, Telugus, Rohillas, and Gonds, liberated Nirmal and surrounding areas, and briefly ran a self-government with Nirmal as its capital.\n*   The British sent a large force under Army Chief Colonel Robert to suppress the rebellion at Nirmal; the British and Nizam armies attacked and tortured the Gonds and Rohillas.\n*   Ramji Gond initially fought a successful guerrilla campaign, but his forces were eventually overpowered by the superior weapons and numbers of the British and Nizam armies.\n*   Ramji Gond was captured along with 1,000 of his soldiers and hanged from a banyan tree on 9 April 1860 at Nirmal, near Khajana Cheruvu Gattu. The tree is since known as **Veyyi Urila Chettu** (or Veyyi Urila Marri).\n\n### Nayakpod and Kolam Rebellion (1879–80)\n\n*   The Nayakpod, Kolam, and other tribal communities of the forests between Bhadrachalam and Rekapalli revolted together during 1879–80.\n*   They revolted jointly against the forest officers and the government because these officers had tripled the tax on Podu cultivation in violation of forest acts.\n\n### Konda Reddy Rebellion (1915–16)\n\n*   The Konda Reddy tribes of the Papikonda forest area, between Bhadrachalam and West Godavari, revolted in 1915–16.\n*   They rose against forest officers and the police for supporting extraction of forest resources by non-tribals with government backing.\n\n### Komaram Bheem Rebellion (1938–40)\n\n**Early life and background**\n\n*   Komaram Bheem, a Gond tribal leader, was born on 22 October 1901 in the forest region of Sankepalli, Jodeghat, Asifabad division of Adilabad district, to Kumram Chinnu and Sombai.\n*   The Gonds and other tribals of the Jodeghat region practised Podu cultivation.\n*   The 1917 forest act, nominally for \"Protection of Tribal Lands,\" instead disrupted tribal life by imposing restrictions and taxes; a Tahsil Office was set up at Utnoor in 1918 to begin collecting forest and revenue taxes.\n*   In 1935, a paper mill was established at Sirpur–Khagaznagar, and large tracts of forest land were taken over for it; tribals who could not resist ended up as labourers in the mill.\n\n**Path to rebellion**\n\n*   Bheem''s family migrated to Surdapur village in Kerimeri mandal, where tribals practised Podu cultivation, but their crop produce was forcibly taken by the Zamindar Siddique.\n*   Bheem revolted against Siddique''s extortion and, in a confrontation, struck him with a stick on the head, killing him instantly.\n*   Facing a Nizam government murder case, Bheem fled to Ballarsha (Maharashtra) with his friend Kondal, then to Chandrapur, where he worked in a printing press under a social worker, Vitthoba, and learned to read and write under his influence.\n*   After Vitthoba''s arrest by the police, Bheem moved to Assam and worked in tea and coffee plantations for five years, where he was inspired by the Indian freedom struggle.\n*   He then returned to Jodeghat in Adilabad to fight for tribal rights.\n\n**The struggle**\n\n*   Bheem made Jodeghat and Babejari his centres of operation and raised the slogan **\"Jal–Jungle–Jameen\"** (Water–Forest–Land).\n*   He fought for the independence of 12 tribal regions under the banner **\"Maavenot–Maaveraj\"** (Maa Gudem lo Maa Rajyam).\n*   Bheem, with four other Gonds, travelled to Hyderabad to seek land titles from the Nizam, Osman Ali Khan, but was not granted an audience.\n*   He wrote several letters to the Divisional Forest Officer and the Sub-Collector detailing the harassment by forest employees, but received no response.\n*   Concluding that armed resistance was the only option, Bheem raised his own army from the youth of the 12 tribal villages of Jodeghat, arming them with conventional weapons and locally made muzzle-loading guns.\n*   He revolted against the local Taluqdar, Sattar, and continued a guerrilla campaign against the Nizam''s forces around Asifabad.\n\n**The Jodeghat encounter and aftermath**\n\n*   On 27 October 1940, acting on information from the informer Kurdupatel, Asifabad Taluqdar Abdul Sattar led an unannounced attack on Bheem''s camp at Jodeghat with 300 armed police.\n*   The police opened indiscriminate fire, killing 140 tribals. Komaram Bheem fought bravely but was killed by the Nizam''s soldiers in the encounter.\n*   Following the Jodeghat incident, the Nizam, Mir Osman Ali Khan, appointed the Austrian ethnologist and London University professor Christopher von Fürer-Haimendorf to study the conditions of the Gonds.\n*   The Gonds are numerically the largest tribal group in the country; their native region was originally Bastar (present-day Chhattisgarh), and in Telangana they are concentrated in Adilabad and Asifabad, where they are known as Raj Gonds.\n*   Haimendorf stayed at Marlawai village to study Gond conditions and later submitted recommendations to the Nizam government.\n*   Based on these recommendations, the Nizam and Revenue Minister Wilfred Gregson introduced the **\"Dastar-Ul-Amal\"** Act in 1940.\n*   Dr. B. R. Ambedkar later framed the rules under the Fifth and Sixth Schedules for the protection of tribal lands as a consequence of the Komaram Bheem struggle, which in turn led to the enactment of the 1/70 Act in India.\n\n**Legacy**\n\n*   Komaram Bheem is revered as the eternal hero of the Gond people; his death anniversary is observed by his heirs and the Gond tribals of Adilabad on the Aswayuja Pournami (full moon) following Dussehra, in October.\n*   The Telangana government has observed Komaram Bheem''s death anniversary as a **\"State Festival\"** since 2014.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter24-peasant-and-tribal-movements-in-telangana-p3", "partName": "Telangana Armed Struggle", "bodyMd": "The Telangana Armed Struggle was conducted between 1946 and 1951 by the Communists, with the central objective of securing land, food, and freedom — **\"Bhoomi, Bhukti, Vimukti.\"** Around 4,000 members were martyred in the course of the struggle, which freed many villages from the atrocities of the Razakars and landlords; peasants fought specifically against the extraction practised by Deshmukhs, Patels, Patwaris, and landlords.\n\n### Main Reasons for the Armed Struggle\n\n**1\\. Landlordism (Feudal System)**  \n\n*   Hundreds of acres of land were concentrated under landlords, with peasants reduced to tenants who had to work the landlord''s fields before cultivating their own.\n*   The scale of landholding among Jagirdars and Zamindars:\n\n| Landlord | Area (acres) |\n|---|---|\n| Janna Reddy Pratap Reddy (Suryapet Deshmukh) | 1,50,000 |\n| Kalluru Zamindar (Khammam) | 1,00,000 |\n| Narsapur Zamindar (Laksettipet) | 50,000 |\n| Visnoor Ramachandra Reddy (Jangoan) | 40,000 |\n\n*   To end this landlordism, peasants launched the armed struggle themselves; \"Sangams\" were later formed in villages by the Communists through the Andhra Mahasabha to organize and support the peasants.\n*   Landlords extorted crop produce in the name of taxes, and peasants additionally had to bribe the Patel, Patwari, and Revenue Inspectors.\n\n2.  **Money-lenders and the Nagu Vaddi System** — under Nagu Vaddi, the loan amount doubled within a year.\n3.  **Hukki Malikana Tax**, levied on toddy workers/tappers.\n4.  **Vetti / Baghela systems** of forced labour.\n5.  **Programmes against Hinduism** (forced conversions).\n6.  **Formation of the Andhra Mahasabha.**\n7.  **Formation of the Telangana Communist Party.**\n\n### Formation of the Telangana Communist Party\n\n*   In December 1939, Maqdoom Mohiuddin, Sayyad Ibrahim, and Raj Bahadur Gour established an organizat' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter24-peasant-and-tribal-movements-in-telangana';
UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || 'ion called the **\"Comrades Association.\"**\n*   In 1940, Raavi Narayana Reddy and Baddam Yella Reddy founded the Communist Party in Hyderabad.\n*   **Important Communist leaders of Hyderabad:** Raavi Narayana Reddy, Baddam Yella Reddy, Maqdoom Mohiuddin, Bheem Reddy Narsimha Reddy, the Arutla Brothers, and Devulapalli Venkateshwar Rao.\n\n### Phases of the Telangana Armed Struggle\n\nFrom the founding of the Communist Party in 1940 to 1951, the Armed Struggle is divided into four phases:\n\n| Phase | Period | Character |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1st | 1940–46 | Consolidation of Communist strength |\n| 2nd | 1946–47 | Revolt against landlords and Deshmukhs (Doras) |\n| 3rd | 1947–48 | Armed rebellion against the Nizam |\n| 4th | 1948–51 | Revolt against the Indian government |\n\n#### Phase 1 (1940–46)\n\n*   Communists conducted \"Education Week\" and \"Beghar Week\" campaigns to build public support and gradually strengthened their base in Hyderabad.\n*   Members of the Andhra Mahasabha organized themselves as \"Sangams\" and went to villages to spread awareness.\n\n**Key event — Shaik Bandagi''s murder:** Bandagi, a poor Muslim farmer of Kamareddy Gudem in Jangaon Taluq, held 4 acres of agricultural land under the Visnoor Deshmukh Rapaka Venkata Ramachandra Reddy. A land dispute arose with relatives Fakir Ahmed, Abbas Ali (who held the post of police patel), and their brothers. The Deshmukh ruled in Abbas Ali''s favour, but Bandagi defied the verdict, approached the local court, and won. Unable to bear the defeat, Abbas Ali and his brothers murdered Bandagi with axes on 26 July 1940, with the Deshmukh''s backing.  \n\n*   This murder became a founding inspiration for the Armed Struggle; the play _**Maa Bhoomi**_ — written by **Sunkara Satyanarayana** and **Vasireddy Bhaskar Rao**, built around the theme \"Dunne Vadide Bhoomi\" (land for the tiller) — opens with a tribute to Shaik Bandagi and was performed extensively in villages during the struggle.\n\n#### Phase 2 (4 July 1946 – 12 June 1947)\n\n**Chakali Ailamma Struggle (Chityala Ailamma)**\n\n*   Chakali Ailamma, of the Rajaka caste, belonged to Palakurthi village in Jangoan Taluq, an area under Visnoor Deshmukh''s control; her husband was Chityala Narsaiah.\n*   Ailamma''s family cultivated 10 acres of wet land and 20 acres of dry land as tenants under Utham Raju Raghava Rao, the Maktedar of Mallempally.\n*   She supported the Andhra Mahasabha and the Communist Party against Visnoor Deshmukh Ramchandra Reddy and police patel Veeramaneni Sheshagiri Rao. In retaliation, the \"Palakurthi Conspiracy Case\" was filed against her husband Narasaiah and sons Somaiah and Lachhaiah, who were arrested with other party leaders.\n*   The Deshmukh seized Ailamma''s leased land, had it re-registered in his own name through the Mallampally Maktedar, and sent goons to seize the crop.\n*   Andhra Mahasabha members Chakilam Yadagiri Rao, Bheem Reddy Narsimha Reddy, and Nalla Pratap Reddy fought off the goons and recovered the crop for Ailamma''s family, and her courage during the episode made her a heroic figure of the movement.\n\n**Palakurthi Conspiracy Case**\n\n*   Andhra Mahasabha leader Arutla Ram Chandra Reddy visited Palakurthi village to inaugurate a village library.\n*   The Visnoor Deshmukh sent goons to disrupt the meeting; the villagers retaliated and severely beat the gang leader, Vanamali Venkaiah.\n*   The angered Deshmukh attacked the village with his goons and the police, filing false cases — including an attempted-murder charge against 12 people, among them Arutla Ramachandra Reddy — an episode remembered as the Palakurthi Conspiracy Case.\n*   **Lawyer for the villagers:** Konda Laxman Bapuji.\n\n**Doddi Komaraiah''s Death**\n\n*   Doddi Komaraiah, of the Kuruma (shepherd) caste, belonged to Kadavendi village in Jangaon Taluq, and was the brother of Doddi Mallaiah, a committee member of the Andhra Mahasabha.\n*   Janakamma (\"Dorasani\"), mother of Visnoor Deshmukh Ramchandra Reddy, lived in Kadavendi and committed many atrocities against the people there.\n*   Andhra Mahasabha leaders Erram Reddy Mohan Reddy, Nalla Narsimhulu, Yerramreddy Kondal Reddy, and organizer Katkuri Ramachandra Reddy revolted against Janakamma; she in turn hired goons under Mishkin Ali to attack Sangam members'' houses.\n*   Sangam members under Erramreddy Mohan Reddy marched with sticks toward the Deshmukh''s Gadi (fort/residence) along with villagers; 23-year-old Doddi Komaraiah joined the march with a stick.\n*   On 4 July 1946, the goons opened indiscriminate fire on the marchers; Doddi Komaraiah was killed, becoming the **first martyr** of the Telangana Armed Struggle.\n*   News of his death drew people from surrounding villages to Kadavendi for retaliatory action; newspapers including the Communist Party–Andhra Maha Sabha press, _Meezan_, _Praja Shakthi_, and _Rehanuma-e-Deccan_ widely reported the martyrdom and the Deshmukh''s tyranny.\n*   Doddi Komaraiah''s death ignited the movement into a full Armed Struggle that continued from 1946 to 1951.\n*   A statue of Doddi Komaraiah has been erected in Moscow by the Russians.\n*   Mashetty Ramachandraiah''s house served as the headquarters of the Kadavendi Andhra Mahasabha Gram Sangham, functioning as the village''s public platform at the time.\n\n**Other incidents of Phase 2**\n\n*   In October 1946, at Balemulla village, Patel Matta Reddy and Garlapati Ananth Reddy died during the revolt.\n\n**Akunur Incident**\n\n*   Akunur village, under Visnoor Deshmukh''s control in Jangaon Taluq, saw police forcibly extract grain from farmers in the name of levy.\n*   The villagers, led by village police patel Sithal Prasad, refused to pay and revolted; a similar incident occurred at Machireddy Palli.\n*   Humiliated officers attacked both villages with police support and molested women there.\n*   The incidents were reported in the national paper _Free Press Journal_ (editor: Sadananda), and Devulapalli Venkateshwar Rao wrote a book on them, _Akunur, Machireddy Palli Duranthalu_.\n*   Mahatma Gandhi, reacting strongly to the police atrocities, asked Padmaja Naidu to investigate and report; she visited both villages with the cooperation of party organizer Sadineni Dharmaiah, criticized the Nizam police''s repressive conduct, and sent her report to Gandhi.\n*   Gandhi then wrote to Hyderabad''s Prime Minister, Sir Akbar Hydari, who appointed a committee of enquiry.\n\n**Errabadu Incident**\n\n*   The Errabadu landlord of Suryapet Taluq, Janna Reddy Pratap Reddy, had annexed 1,000 acres from surrounding farmers.\n*   Farmers revolted against him under the leadership of Gajula Ramachandraiah of Erravelli village.\n\n**Cultural mobilization and state repression**\n\n*   Communists mobilized public support through Burra Katha, Oggu Katha, and the _Maa Bhoomi_ drama; the government subsequently banned the drama.\n*   In October 1946, the government set up military camps at Warangal and Nalgonda to suppress Communist activity, prompting the Communists to shift their base to Vijayawada — known during this period as \"Stalingrad.\"\n*   In November 1946, the Nizam government banned the Communist Party and appointed a commission, chaired by Mirza Ali Hyder, to suppress the Armed Struggle.\n\n#### Phase 3 (12 June 1947 – 17 September 1948)\n\n*   India became independent on 15 August 1947, but the Nizam declared that Hyderabad State would remain independent, joining neither India nor Pakistan. The peasants'' struggle against the feudal system thus moved into a new stage — resistance against the Nizam, aimed at merging Hyderabad into India.\n*   On 12 June 1947, Nizam Mir Osman Ali declared himself a sovereign independent ruler and suppressed those who opposed him.\n*   **Effect:** on 11 September 1947, the Communists announced the start of armed struggle against the Nizam. The declaration was made by Raavi Narayana Reddy, Baddam Yella Reddy, and Maqdoom Mohiuddin.\n\n**Objectives of the Armed Struggle**\n\n*   Seizing weapons from landlords, police, and Razakars.\n*   Occupying landlord and government wasteland for distribution among the poor.\n*   Obstructing government functioning by refusing to pay taxes.\n*   Boycotting government offices, courts, and educational institutions, while expanding Communist Party training.\n*   Hoisting the national flag and the Communist flag to declare Telangana liberated.\n*   Establishing guerrilla camps at the district, taluq, and village level to protect the people, with the ultimate aim of abolishing Nizam rule and the feudal system.\n\n**Three-tier organizational strategy** — the Communists organized villagers into three groups:\n\n1.  **Village Protection Group** — formed of enthusiastic young men who patrolled village outskirts from trees, signalled the village through distinctive sounds when Razakars or Nizam police approached, and confronted them using gathered Barmars (shells), spears, and common weapons.\n2.  **Abolition Group (Nirmulana Dalam)** — tasked with blocking enemy entry into villages, including by destroying bridges to stop Razakars and Nizam soldiers.\n3.  **Guerrilla Group** — equipped with modern weapons and guns, this group retaliated against landlords, Razakars, and police, attacking Deshmukh houses and police camps; in some villages it succeeded in dismantling government authority and establishing local self-rule.\n\n**Key engagements**\n\n*   The guerrillas'' first attack was on the police camp at Ravula Penta, aimed at seizing weapons; it failed, and leaders Venkat Reddy and Devabathini Venkateshwar Rao were killed.\n*   On 29 November 1947, around 6,000 people under Asireddy Narsimha Reddy attacked the large Razakar camp of Jagirdar Kolanpaka in Bhuvanagiri Taluq and hoisted the red flag there.\n*   Bheem Reddy Narsimha Reddy initiated the **\"Udara\"** programme to clear Razakar and soldier camps: fires were set to smoke out occupants, who were then disarmed as they emerged for air.\n*   People''s guerrillas bombed Kotapadu village, killing five Razakars and seven policemen, including landlord Vangeti Linga Reddy for aiding the Razakars and police.\n*   Anabheri Prabhakar Rao (Karimnagar) and Bhupathi Reddy (Siricilla) took control of villages and ran parallel governments during this phase.\n*   In January 1948, Anabheri Prabhakar Rao and Singireddy Bhupati Reddy, along with 14 others, were killed in counter-fire at Mandapuram village, Karimnagar district.\n\n**Guerrilla leaders martyred in Phase 3:**\n\n| Leader | Region |\n|---|---|\n| Anabheri Prabhakar Rao | Karimnagar |\n| Bhupathi Reddy | Siricilla |\n| Renikunta Rami Reddy | Bhuvanagiri |\n| Pasunuri Venkat Reddy | Nalgonda |\n| Erra Satyam | Huzur Nagar |\n| Gopal Reddy | Suryapet |\n\n*   On 29 November 1947, Nizam Osman Ali Khan signed a Standstill Agreement with the Indian government.\n*   On 17 September 1948, Hyderabad State was merged into the Indian Union through **Operation Polo**, closing the third phase.\n\n#### Phase 4 (17 September 1948 – 21 October 1951)\n\n*   Following a call from national Communist Party secretary Randive, the Armed Struggle continued from 17 September 1948, now directed against the Indian government.\n*   Indian Home Minister Vallabhbhai Patel visited Hyderabad on 6 October 1950 and, addressing a meeting at Fateh Maidan, declared that there would not be a single Communist left on Telangana soil.\n*   J. N. Chaudhuri, Military Governor of Hyderabad, was given full powers to suppress the Communists.\n*   The military government drew up the **\"Briggs Plan\"** under Nanjappa to suppress the movement; Nanjappa subsequently killed many Communists indiscriminately.\n\n**Conclusion of the Armed Struggle**\n\n*   On 21 October 1951, on the advice of Soviet Communist Party leader Stalin, the Communists called off the armed struggle.\n\n### Women in the Armed Struggle\n\n*   Women from the Koya and Chenchu tribal communities supported the guerrilla forces and Communist cadres throughout the struggle.\n*   The first woman martyr of the Armed Struggle was **Ramulamma**, an 11-year-old girl from Mangalpally village in Nalgonda Taluq, who died from torture at the hands of the Nizam''s police and Razakars.\n*   Other notable women participants: Mallu Swarajyam (Nalgonda), Arutla Kamala Devi (Bhuvanagiri), Lalitha (Suryapet), along with Raavi Sitha Devi, Brij Rani Gaur, Thai, and Sarpu Behan.\n\n**Mallu Swarajyam**\n\n*   Born in 1931 in Kothagudem village, Thungathurthy mandal, Suryapet district; her family, inspired by the freedom movement''s \"Swaraj\" slogan, named her Swarajyam.\n*   She was drawn into revolutionary politics after reading Maxim Gorky''s novel _Mother_.\n*   Responding to the Andhra Mahasabha''s call, she fought against Vetti Chakiri (forced labour).\n*   She went on to command a Dalam (armed squad) fighting the Zamindars, prompting the Nizam government to place a reward of Rs. 10,000 on her.\n*   Her husband, Mallu Venkat Narsimha Reddy, and her brother, Bheem Reddy Narsimha Reddy, were both influences on her.\n*   As part of the struggle, she took land from Zamindars for distribution among the poor and used folk songs to mobilize villagers.\n*   She was elected to the Andhra Pradesh Legislative Assembly from the Thungathurthy constituency in 1978 and 1983.\n\n### Literature of the Armed Struggle\n\n**Books on the Armed Struggle:**\n\n| Author | Work |\n|---|---|\n| Devulapalli Venkateshwara Rao | History of the People''s Armed Struggle of Telangana (Telangana Prajala Sayudha Porata Charita) |\n| Puchalapalli Sundarayya | Telangana People''s Struggle & Its Lessons; Veera Telangana Viplava Poratam, Guna Patalu |\n| Devulapally Ramanuja Rao | History of Telangana Armed Struggle |\n| Nalla Narsimhulu | Telangana Armed Struggle – My Experiences |\n| Madiraju Ramakoteshwar Rao | Telangana Abhyudhaya Amshalu (Progress Factors) |\n| Raavi Narayana Reddy | Veera Telangana, Naa Anubhavalu Gnapakalu (My Experiences – Memories) |\n| Arutla Ram Chandra Reddy | Telangana Porata Smruthulu |\n| Chandra Rajeshwar Rao | Veera Telangana Viplava Poratam |\n| Ayyapu Venkata Ramanaiah | Naji Naijam |\n| Maqdoom Mohiuddin | Haveli |\n| Kundurthi Anjaneyulu | Telangana, Nagaramlo Vana |\n| K. V. Ramana Reddy | Bhuvana Gosha, Adavi (Forest) |\n| Vattikota Alwar Swamy | Telangana |\n| Krishna Prasad | Simha Garjana (novel) |\n| Mahendar Ram Mohan Rao | Onamalu, Radha Chakralu, Mruthyuvu Needalo (novel) |\n\n**Notable contemporary comments/slogans:**\n\n*   Dasarathi Krishnamacharya — \"Maa Nizam Raju Janma Janmala Buju\"\n*   Rentala Gopala Krishna — \"Pagaleyi Nizam Kota, Egarey Erabavuta\" (Destroy the Nizam Fort, hoist the red flag)\n*   Raavi Narayana Reddy — \"Maa Nizam Rajyam Kalthi Leni Madya Yugapu Bhuswamya Vyavasta\"\n*   Chandra Rajeshwar Rao — the \"Andhra Thesis\" slogan\n*   Kaloji — \"Manakompalarchina, Manalni Champajusina, Manava Adamulanu Mandalidheeshulanu Kalambu Ragane Katesi Teerale\"\n\n##' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter24-peasant-and-tribal-movements-in-telangana';
UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || '# Results of the Telangana Armed Struggle\n\n*   Village protection forces helped politically enlighten the peasantry.\n*   The struggle freed people from the domination of Zamindars and Razakars.\n*   Landlordism (feudalism) was substantially altered.\n*   Vetti and other illegal taxes and bribery practices were abolished.\n*   Land reforms were introduced in 1949 as a direct result of Communist efforts.\n*   The Tenancy Act was passed in June 1950, granting ownership rights to tenants cultivating Deshmukhs'' lands.\n*   The struggle brought agrarian revolution to national attention and became a model for other peasant struggles across India, eventually pushing the Congress government to bring in land reforms.", "free": false}]' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter24-peasant-and-tribal-movements-in-telangana';
